800:
(a) The following public officer(s) are designated to enforce the provisions this article:
(1) The chief Building Official;
(2) The fire chief;
(3) The police chief;
(4) Any code enforcement officer; or
(5) Any person/entity specially appointed and designated by any one (1) of the above officials.
(b) The designated public officers shall be empowered to:
(1) Investigate the hotels, motels, and extended-stay hotels, and multi-family rental properties to determine whether they are unfit for human habitation or are unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use or are vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes;
(2) Enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations; provided, however, that such entries shall be made in such manner as to conform to all state and federal laws;
(3) Issue citations for violations of the provisions of this article, optional building, fire, life safety, and other codes adopted by ordinance, and existence of conditions creating a public health hazard or general nuisance and seek to enforce such citations;
(4) Appoint and fix the duties of such officers, agents, and employees as he or she deems necessary to carry out the purposes of this article; and
(5) Delegate any of his or her functions and powers under this article to such officers and agents as he or she may designate.
(c) If the hotel, motel, or extended-stay hotel, or multi-family rental property responsible party fails to comply with any of the provisions of this article, the city may impose a fine in accordance with the following schedule:
(1) First conviction in a calendar year: A minimum of two hundred and fifty dollars ($250.00);
(2) Second conviction in a 12-month period measured from the date of the first conviction: A minimum of five hundred dollars ($500.00);
(3) Third conviction in a 12-month period measured from the date of the first conviction: A minimum of seven hundred and fifty dollars ($750.00); and
(4) Fourth conviction in a 12-month period measured from the date of the first conviction: A minimum of one thousand dollars ($1,000.00).
(d) If a general nuisance of the hotel, motel, or extended-stay hotel, or multi-family rental property is found to be determined by the designated public officials stated herein, then nuisance abatement proceedings shall occur under article iv of this chapter.
(Ord. No. 2024-14, § 3(Exh. B), 10-10-24; Ord. No. 2025-22, § 1(Exh. A), 10-6-25)

805:
It shall be unlawful for any person to:
(1) Establish, manage, operate, maintain, rent, lease, license, advertise, list, market, offer, solicit bookings for, or otherwise hold out any property as a prohibited transient lodging establishment;
(2) Accept or solicit reservations, deposits, payments, or other consideration for transient occupancy;
(3) Provide keys, lockbox combinations, keypad codes, or other means of access to transient occupants;
(4) Install, maintain, or utilize lockboxes, keypad locks, or similar devices for the purpose of facilitating transient occupancy;
(5) Employ or engage a host, property manager, agent, or other third party to market or operate a prohibited transient lodging establishment;
(6) Knowingly occupy or possess any property in a manner that constitutes a prohibited transient lodging establishment;
(7) Permit any person to occupy or possess a property in a manner that constitutes a prohibited transient lodging establishment; or
(8) Following written notice from the city that a property is being unlawfully used or offered as a prohibited transient lodging establishment, continue to publish or maintain a commercial advertisement, listing, reservation platform, or booking mechanism offering transient occupancy in violation of this article after expiration of the compliance period stated in the written notice.
(Ord. No. 2026-14, § 1(Exh. A), 6-15-26)

806:
(a) The following may be considered by the city as competent evidence of a violation of this article:
(1) Advertisements, listings, or postings on websites, television, radio, mobile applications, social media platforms, or similar services;
(2) Screenshots, recordings, invoices, receipts, archived webpages, booking calendars, pricing information, guest reviews, or photographs;
(3) Reservation confirmations, advertisements, or promotional materials;
(4) The presence of lockboxes, keypad entry systems, guest books, checkout instructions, or similar materials, when considered together with other evidence indicating that the property is being used or offered as a prohibited transient lodging establishment;
(5) Frequent turnover of occupants or vehicles;
(6) Statements, complaints, or other disclosures from neighbors, occupants, guests, or other witnesses;
(7) Testimony by any person, when given in an administrative or judicial proceeding against another person for violation of any provision of this section;
(8) Utility usage patterns reasonably indicating repeated transient occupancy;
(9) Payment records or evidence of consideration received or remitted;
(10) Parking congestion, illegally parked vehicles, blocked driveways, blocked fire hydrants, or obstruction of sidewalks; and
(11) Any other competent evidence tending to show that the property is being used or offered as a prohibited transient lodging establishment.
(c) Advertisements, listings, offers, booking calendars, guest reviews, pricing information, reservation confirmations, and similar materials may be considered by the city as competent evidence, together with all other relevant evidence, in determining whether a property is being used, operated, maintained, or offered as a prohibited transient lodging establishment in violation of this article.
(d) This article is intended to regulate the unlawful use, operation, maintenance, offering, and occupancy of property as a prohibited transient lodging establishment and not to restrict constitutionally protected speech, assembly, or activities. The provisions of this article are directed toward conduct and land use activities occurring within the city and not the content of any communication. Advertisements, listings, postings, and similar communications may be considered as evidence together with other competent evidence in determining whether a violation exists but shall not, standing alone, establish a violation. This article shall be interpreted and applied in a manner consistent with the United States Constitution, the Constitution of the State of Georgia, and all other applicable law.
(Ord. No. 2026-14, § 1(Exh. A), 6-15-26)

817:
(a) All dumpsters, compactors, oversized containers, and other bulk refuse containers shall be located within a dumpster enclosure.
(b) Each dumpster enclosure shall:
(1) Be constructed of masonry, decorative metal, wood, composite material, or other durable material approved by the building official;
(2) Be of sufficient height to completely screen the refuse container from public view from adjacent public streets and rights-of-way;
(3) Include opaque gates capable of fully enclosing the refuse container area;
(4) Be maintained in good repair and free from graffiti, deterioration, broken components, and structural defects; and
(5) Provide unobstructed access for collection vehicles.
(c) Roll-off containers utilized for construction, demolition, emergency cleanup, or temporary projects may remain on-site without a permanent enclosure for a period not exceeding thirty (30) days unless extended by the building official for good cause shown.
(Ord. No. 2026-15, § 2(Exh. B), 6-15-26)

818:
Property owners, occupants, tenants, managers, and persons responsible for the operation of refuse containers shall:
(1) Keep all lids, doors, and covers closed except when refuse is actively being deposited or collected;
(2) Maintain dumpster and refuse container areas free from litter, debris, grease, liquids, odors, insects, rodents, vermin, and other unsanitary conditions;
(3) Promptly remove refuse deposited outside a refuse container;
(4) Maintain all enclosure walls, gates, pads, and surrounding areas in a clean and sanitary condition;
(5) Ensure refuse containers are not overflowing;
(6) Maintain refuse collection service at a frequency sufficient to prevent overflow or accumulation of waste;
(7) Prevent any condition constituting a nuisance, health hazard, or threat to public safety; and
(8) Comply with all applicable provisions of chapter 10 relating to refuse collection and solid waste disposal.
(Ord. No. 2026-15, § 2(Exh. B), 6-15-26)

819:
(a) It shall be unlawful for any person to:
(1) Maintain a dumpster, compactor, oversized container, or other bulk refuse container without a required enclosure;
(2) Permit a dumpster enclosure to fall into disrepair;
(3) Permit refuse, garbage, trash, or debris to accumulate outside a refuse container;
(4) Permit a refuse container to overflow;
(5) Store refuse in a manner that attracts rodents, insects, vermin, or other pests; or
(6) Maintain a dumpster or refuse container area in a manner that constitutes a nuisance or health hazard.
(7) Permit the accumulation of litter, refuse, garbage, rubbish, discarded materials, vegetation, or debris in or around a dumpster enclosure or refuse container area.
(b) Violations of this article shall be punishable in accordance with section 1-8 of this Code. Each day a violation continues shall constitute a separate offense.
(c) Notwithstanding section 1-8, the minimum fines for violations of this article shall be:
(1) First offense: not less than two hundred fifty dollars ($250.00);
(2) Second offense occurring within twelve (12) months of a prior conviction under this article: not less than five hundred dollars ($500.00);
(3) Third or subsequent offense occurring within twelve (12) months of a prior conviction under this article shall be punishable by a mandatory fine of one thousand dollars ($1,000.00).
(d) The remedies provided herein shall be cumulative and shall not limit the city's authority to pursue nuisance abatement, injunctive relief, cost recovery, or any other remedy authorized by law.
(Ord. No. 2026-15, § 2(Exh. B), 6-15-26)

866:
(a) Persons may irrigate outdoors daily for purposes of planting, growing, managing, or maintaining ground cover, trees, shrubs, or other plants only between the hours of 4:00 p.m. and 10:00 a.m.
(b) Paragraph (a) of this section shall not create any limitation upon the following outdoor water uses:
(1) Commercial agricultural operations as defined in O.C.G.A. § 1-3-3;
(2) Capture and reuse of cooling system condensate or storm water in compliance with applicable local ordinances and state guidelines;
(3) Reuse of gray water in compliance with O.C.G.A. § 31-3-5.2 and applicable local board of health regulations adopted pursuant thereto;
(4) Use of reclaimed waste water by a designated user from a system permitted by the environmental protection division of the department to provide reclaimed waste water;
(5) Irrigation of personal food gardens;
(6) Irrigation of new and replanted plant, seed, or turf in landscapes, golf courses, or sports turf fields during installation and for a period of thirty (30) days immediately following the date of installation;
(7) Drip irrigation or irrigation using soaker hoses;
(8) Handwatering with a hose with automatic cutoff or handheld container;
(9) Use of water withdrawn from private water wells or surface water by an owner or operator of property if such well or surface water is on said property;
(10) Irrigation of horticultural crops held for sale, resale, or installation;
(11) Irrigation of athletic fields, golf courses, or public turf grass recreational areas;
(12) Installation, maintenance, or calibration of irrigation systems; or
(13) Hydroseeding.
(c) (1) No person shall use or allow the use of water in violation of the restrictions on outdoor water use contained in this section.
(2) The code enforcement officer(s) shall be the enforcement authority for this section, and the city manager may, as he deems necessary, authorize other departments or employees to enforce this section.
(3) Any person who violates any provision of this section shall, upon conviction by the municipal court be punished as provided in section 1-8 of this Code.
(Ord. No. 2010-23, § 1, 12-6-10)
